# Credentials only. What to talk to lives in aibench.yaml — each profile
# names its credentials file (auth.credentials: .env.myendpoint).
#
# Pick EXACTLY ONE method below — two is a named error, not a silent
# winner. A custom header is separate and always sent on top of it.
#
# Reference: docs/authentication.md

# --- API key, openai provider — sent as Authorization: Bearer ------------
#OPENAI_API_KEY=

# --- API key, anthropic provider — sent as x-api-key ---------------------
# AZURE_API_KEY is what a Foundry Claude deployment issues.
#ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
#AZURE_API_KEY=

# --- Entra ID, your identity: a token you minted -------------------------
# az login, then mint one — the --resource is the audience the route wants
# (https://ai.azure.com for the responses api and agents):
#   az account get-access-token --resource https://ai.azure.com \
#     --query accessToken --output tsv
# Sent as Authorization: Bearer; expires on its own (~1h), nothing here
# refreshes it. No api.headers line needed.
#AZURE_TOKEN=

# --- Entra ID, your identity: let aibench mint from your az login --------
# The alternative to AZURE_TOKEN — never both. Opting in is explicit:
# without this the app never reaches for your sign-in. Fresh token every
# run, so nothing to refresh by hand.
#AZURE_USE_LOGIN=true

# --- Entra ID, app identity (service principal) --------------------------
# A registered app's own identity, not yours: CI, automation, shared
# endpoints. All three or none.
#AZURE_TENANT_ID=
#AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
#AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- Custom header — composes with any one method above ------------------
# Referenced from the profile: headers: { api-key: ${FOUNDRY_KEY} }
#FOUNDRY_KEY=
